1
00:02:39,428 --> 00:02:45,428
Change your fate.

2
00:05:18,857 --> 00:05:24,857
Brave is a animation, adventure, comedy film directed by Mark Andrews.

3
00:07:58,285 --> 00:08:04,285
In the mystical Scottish Highlands, Merida is the princess of a
kingdom ruled by King Fergus and Queen Elinor.

4
00:10:37,714 --> 00:10:43,714
An unruly daughter and an accomplished archer, Merida one day defies a sacred
custom of the land and inadvertently brings turmoil to the kingdom.

5
00:13:17,142 --> 00:13:23,142
In an attempt to set things right, Merida seeks out an eccentric
old Wise Woman and is granted an ill-fated wish.

6
00:15:56,571 --> 00:16:02,571
Also figuring into Merida's quest — and serving as comic relief — are the kingdom's three lords: the
enormous Lord MacGuffin, the surly Lord Macintosh, and the disagreeable Lord Dingwall.

7
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:42,000
Merida (voice), played by Kelly Macdonald.

8
00:21:15,428 --> 00:21:21,428
Elinor (voice), played by Emma Thompson.

9
00:23:54,857 --> 00:24:00,857
Fergus (voice), played by Billy Connolly.

10
00:26:34,285 --> 00:26:40,285
The Witch (voice), played by Julie Walters.

11
00:29:13,714 --> 00:29:19,714
Lord Dingwall (voice), played by Robbie Coltrane.

12
00:31:53,142 --> 00:31:59,142
Lord MacGuffin / Young MacGuffin (voice), played by Kevin McKidd.

13
00:34:32,571 --> 00:34:38,571
Brave is a 2012 American animated fantasy adventure film produced by Pixar
Animation Studios and released by Walt Disney Pictures.

14
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:18,000
The film was directed by Mark Andrews and Brenda Chapman, co-directed by Steve Purcell, and produced by
Katherine Sarafian, with John Lasseter, Andrew Stanton, and Pete Docter serving as executive producers.

15
00:39:51,428 --> 00:39:57,428
The story was written by Chapman, who also co-wrote the film's
screenplay with Andrews, Purcell, and Irene Mecchi.

16
00:42:30,857 --> 00:42:36,857
The film stars the voices of Kelly Macdonald, Billy Connolly, Emma Thompson,
Julie Walters, Robbie Coltrane, Kevin McKidd, and Craig Ferguson.

17
00:45:10,285 --> 00:45:16,285
Set in the Scottish Highlands, the film tells the story of Princess Merida of DunBroch (Macdonald) who defies
an age-old custom, causing chaos in the kingdom by expressing the desire not to be betrothed.

18
00:47:49,714 --> 00:47:55,714
When Queen Elinor (Thompson), her mother, is turned into a bear, Merida must
look within herself and find the key to saving the kingdom.

19
00:50:29,142 --> 00:50:35,142
Merida is the first character in the Disney
Princess line to be created by Pixar.

20
00:53:08,571 --> 00:53:14,571
The film is also dedicated to Pixar chairman and Apple co-founder and
CEO Steve Jobs, who died before the film's release.

21
00:55:47,999 --> 00:55:53,999
Brave is Pixar's first film with a female protagonist, and the first one
animated with a new proprietary animation system, called Presto.

22
00:58:27,428 --> 00:58:33,428
Originally titled The Bear and the Bow, the film was first announced
in April 2008 alongside Up (2009) and Cars 2 (2011).

23
01:01:06,857 --> 01:01:12,857
Chapman, who had just wrapped up work as a story artist on Cars (2006), drew
inspiration for the film's story from her relationship with her own daughter.

24
01:03:46,285 --> 01:03:52,285
Co-directing with Andrews and Purcell, Chapman became Pixar's
first female director of a feature-length film.

25
01:06:25,714 --> 01:06:31,714
To create the most complex visuals possible, Pixar completely rewrote
their animation system for the first time in 25 years.

26
01:09:05,142 --> 01:09:11,142
Brave is the first film to use the Dolby Atmos sound format.

27
01:11:44,571 --> 01:11:50,571
The filmmakers created three original tartan patterns
for three of the four clans in the film.

28
01:14:24,000 --> 01:14:30,000
Patrick Doyle composed the film's musical score.

29
01:17:03,428 --> 01:17:09,428
Brave premiered at the Seattle International Film Festival on June 10, 2012,
and was theatrically released in North America on June 22.

30
01:19:42,857 --> 01:19:48,857
Receiving generally positive reviews, it was a box office success,
grossing $539 million against a $185 million budget.

31
01:22:22,285 --> 01:22:28,285
The film won the Academy Award, the Golden Globe, and the
BAFTA Award for Best Animated Feature Film.

32
01:25:01,714 --> 01:25:07,714
Preceding the feature theatrically was a short film
entitled La Luna, directed by Enrico Casarosa.

33
01:27:41,142 --> 01:27:47,142
The film explores themes of witch, kingdom, scotland, rebel, bravery.

34
01:30:20,571 --> 01:30:26,571
The tone is lighthearted.
